    "evidence_text": "We introduce an additive modification to the objective function of the embedding learning algorithm that encourages the embedding vectors of words that are semantically related to a predefined concept to take larger values along a specified dimension, while leaving the original semantic learning mechanism mostly unaffected.\n\nAdditionally, the top-50 words that achieve the greatest 32nd dimension values among the considered 1000 words are emphasized with enlarged markers, along with text annotations.\n\nIn the presented simulation of the proposed algorithm, the 32nd dimension values are encoded with the concept JUDGMENT, which is reflected as an increase in the dimension values for words such as committee, academy, and article.\n\nto the concept word-group for that dimension.\n\nWe also present the list of words with the greatest dimension value for the dimensions 11, 13, 16, 31, 36, 39, 41, 43 and 79 in Table TABREF11 .\n\nThis again illustrates that a semantic concept can indeed be coded to a vector dimension provided that a sensible lexical resource is used to guide semantically related words to the desired vector dimension via the proposed objective function in ( SECREF4 ).\n\nThey also reflect the associated concepts at their\n\nOur",
